    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old man sustained a mechanical fall 3 months ago without fracture. Since then, he has been progressively restricting his activities, avoiding going outside, and is fearful of falling again. He now requires assistance for ambulation even though he has no physical injury or neurologic deficit. This has led to deconditioning, muscle atrophy, and social isolation. Which of the following best describes this condition and the most appropriate intervention?', '["Normal aging; all older adults naturally reduce activity after age 70, and no intervention is needed", "Post-fall syndrome (fear of falling / ptophobia); this is a recognized geriatric syndrome characterized by excessive fear of falling leading to activity restriction, deconditioning, loss of independence, and paradoxically increased fall risk; management includes cognitive behavioral therapy, supervised physical therapy with progressive balance and strength training, and occupational therapy for home safety modifications", "Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD); start high-dose benzodiazepines for anxiety management", "Agoraphobia; this is a primary psychiatric disorder that requires antipsychotic medication as first-line treatment", "Malingering; the patient is intentionally restricting activities for secondary gain and should be confronted directly"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Post-fall syndrome** (also called **ptophobia** or **fear of falling**) is a recognized and common geriatric syndrome affecting **30-73% of older adults who have fallen** and even 12-65% of those who have never fallen. It creates a **vicious cycle**: Fear of falling -> Activity restriction -> Physical deconditioning, muscle atrophy, loss of balance -> Increased fall risk -> Further falls -> Greater fear. This cycle leads to progressive loss of independence, social isolation, depression, and institutionalization. Management: (1) **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)** to address fear and self-efficacy, (2) **Supervised physical therapy** with progressive balance and strength training (e.g., tai chi), (3) **Occupational therapy** for home safety modifications and adaptive strategies, (4) Treatment of underlying conditions (e.g., orthostatic hypotension, vision impairment). Benzodiazepines are **contraindicated** (Beers Criteria) as they increase fall risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics; JAGS 2011)'),
