    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old male sustained a mechanical fall 8 months ago without fracture. Since then, he has been progressively restricting his activities, avoiding going outside, and is fearful of falling again. He now requires assistance for ambulation even though he has no physical injury or neurologic deficit. This has led to deconditioning, muscle atrophy, and social isolation. Which of the following best describes this condition and the most appropriate intervention?', '["Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD); start high-dose benzodiazepines for anxiety management", "Malingering; the patient is intentionally restricting activities for secondary gain and should be confronted directly", "Post-fall syndrome (fear of falling / ptophobia); this is a recognized geriatric syndrome characterized by excessive fear of falling leading to activity restriction, deconditioning, loss of independence, and paradoxically increased fall risk; management includes cognitive behavioral therapy, supervised physical therapy with progressive balance and strength training, and occupational therapy for home safety modifications", "Normal aging; all older adults naturally reduce activity after age 70, and no intervention is needed", "Agoraphobia; this is a primary psychiatric disorder that requires antipsychotic medication as first-line treatment"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Post-fall syndrome** (also called **ptophobia** or **fear of falling**) is a recognized and common geriatric syndrome affecting **30-73% of older adults who have fallen** and even 12-65% of those who have never fallen. It creates a **vicious cycle**: Fear of falling -> Activity restriction -> Physical deconditioning, muscle atrophy, loss of balance -> Increased fall risk -> Further falls -> Greater fear. This cycle leads to progressive loss of independence, social isolation, depression, and institutionalization. Management: (1) **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)** to address fear and self-efficacy, (2) **Supervised physical therapy** with progressive balance and strength training (e.g., tai chi), (3) **Occupational therapy** for home safety modifications and adaptive strategies, (4) Treatment of underlying conditions (e.g., orthostatic hypotension, vision impairment). Benzodiazepines are **contraindicated** (Beers Criteria) as they increase fall risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics; JAGS 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old male presents after he fell 2 times in the past year. He has hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and takes 8 medications daily. According to the AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines, which of the following components should be included in a comprehensive multifactorial fall risk assessment?', '["No assessment is needed for community-dwelling older adults with recurrent falls, as falls are a normal part of aging", "Only an ECG to evaluate for cardiac arrhythmias as the sole cause of falls", "Only a brain MRI to rule out a structural lesion causing the falls", "Gait and balance assessment (e.g., Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure measurement, visual acuity testing, medication review (especially CNS-active drugs, antihypertensives), lower extremity strength and sensation testing, footwear evaluation, cognitive screening, and home hazard assessment", "Only a medication count; falls are always caused by polypharmacy and no other evaluation is needed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Fall Prevention** recommend a **multifactorial fall risk assessment** for all older adults who report **>= 2 falls in 12 months, a single fall with injury, or gait/balance difficulties**. The assessment should include: (1) **Gait, balance, and mobility** (Timed Up and Go [TUG], Berg Balance Scale), (2) **Orthostatic blood pressure** (supine to standing), (3) **Visual acuity** (cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma), (4) **Medication review** (especially **CNS-active drugs**: benzodiazepines, opioids, anticonvulsants, antidepressants; antihypertensives causing orthostasis), (5) **Lower extremity strength, proprioception, and sensation** (peripheral neuropathy), (6) **Footwear evaluation**, (7) **Cognitive screening** (dementia increases fall risk 2-3x), (8) **Home safety/hazard assessment** (loose rugs, poor lighting, absence of grab bars), (9) **Cardiovascular assessment** (arrhythmias, carotid sinus hypersensitivity). (Ref: AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old female presents after she fell 3 times in the past year. She has hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and takes 10 medications daily. According to the AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines, which of the following components should be included in a comprehensive multifactorial fall risk assessment?', '["No assessment is needed for community-dwelling older adults with recurrent falls, as falls are a normal part of aging", "Gait and balance assessment (e.g., Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure measurement, visual acuity testing, medication review (especially CNS-active drugs, antihypertensives), lower extremity strength and sensation testing, footwear evaluation, cognitive screening, and home hazard assessment", "Only a medication count; falls are always caused by polypharmacy and no other evaluation is needed", "Only an ECG to evaluate for cardiac arrhythmias as the sole cause of falls", "Only a brain MRI to rule out a structural lesion causing the falls"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Fall Prevention** recommend a **multifactorial fall risk assessment** for all older adults who report **>= 2 falls in 12 months, a single fall with injury, or gait/balance difficulties**. The assessment should include: (1) **Gait, balance, and mobility** (Timed Up and Go [TUG], Berg Balance Scale), (2) **Orthostatic blood pressure** (supine to standing), (3) **Visual acuity** (cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma), (4) **Medication review** (especially **CNS-active drugs**: benzodiazepines, opioids, anticonvulsants, antidepressants; antihypertensives causing orthostasis), (5) **Lower extremity strength, proprioception, and sensation** (peripheral neuropathy), (6) **Footwear evaluation**, (7) **Cognitive screening** (dementia increases fall risk 2-3x), (8) **Home safety/hazard assessment** (loose rugs, poor lighting, absence of grab bars), (9) **Cardiovascular assessment** (arrhythmias, carotid sinus hypersensitivity). (Ref: AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
