 AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female presents after she had 4 falls in the past year. She has hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and takes 10 medications daily. According to the AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines, which of the following components should be included in a comprehensive multifactorial fall risk assessment?', '["Only an ECG to evaluate for cardiac arrhythmias as the sole cause of falls", "Only a medication count; falls are always caused by polypharmacy and no other evaluation is needed", "Gait and balance assessment (e.g., Timed Up and Go), orthostatic blood pressure measurement, visual acuity testing, medication review (especially CNS-active drugs, antihypertensives), lower extremity strength and sensation testing, footwear evaluation, cognitive screening, and home hazard assessment", "No assessment is needed for community-dwelling older adults with recurrent falls, as falls are a normal part of aging", "Only a brain MRI to rule out a structural lesion causing the falls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Fall Prevention** recommend a **multifactorial fall risk assessment** for all older adults who report **>= 2 falls in 12 months, a single fall with injury, or gait/balance difficulties**. The assessment should include: (1) **Gait, balance, and mobility** (Timed Up and Go [TUG], Berg Balance Scale), (2) **Orthostatic blood pressure** (supine to standing), (3) **Visual acuity** (cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma), (4) **Medication review** (especially **CNS-active drugs**: benzodiazepines, opioids, anticonvulsants, antidepressants; antihypertensives causing orthostasis), (5) **Lower extremity strength, proprioception, and sensation** (peripheral neuropathy), (6) **Footwear evaluation**, (7) **Cognitive screening** (dementia increases fall risk 2-3x), (8) **Home safety/hazard assessment** (loose rugs, poor lighting, absence of grab bars), (9) **Cardiovascular assessment** (arrhythmias, carotid sinus hypersensitivity). (Ref: AGS/BGS 2010 Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female is brought in after her 2nd fall in 5 months. Medication review reveals: amitriptyline 25 mg at bedtime for neuropathic pain, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["Discontinue amitriptyline (tricyclic antidepressant with strong anticholinergic properties); TCAs are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to high fall risk from orthostatic hypotension, sedation, and anticholinergic effects (confusion, blurred vision, urinary retention); consider duloxetine or gabapentin as safer alternatives for neuropathic pain", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs)** (amitriptyline, nortriptyline, imipramine) are highly anticholinergic and cause **orthostatic hypotension, excessive sedation, cognitive impairment, and blurred vision** - all of which increase fall risk. Amitriptyline is the most anticholinergic TCA and is **specifically listed in the Beers Criteria as a high-risk medication**. Safer alternatives for neuropathic pain include **duloxetine** (SNRI), **gabapentin** or **pregabalin** (though these also require caution for sedation), or topical agents (lidocaine patches, capsaicin). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female is brought in after her 4th fall in 9 months. Medication review reveals: lorazepam 1 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Taper and discontinue lorazepam (benzodiazepine); benzodiazepines are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate for older adults due to significantly increased fall risk, cognitive impairment, sedation, and delirium; they are the highest-priority medication class to discontinue for fall prevention"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Benzodiazepines** (lorazepam, diazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam) are among the **highest-risk medication classes for falls** in older adults. The AGS Beers Criteria strongly recommends **avoiding benzodiazepines** in older adults due to: (1) **Increased fall risk** (OR 1.4-1.8), (2) **Cognitive impairment**, (3) **Excessive sedation and psychomotor impairment**, (4) **Delirium risk**, and (5) **Fracture risk** (especially hip fractures). Non-pharmacologic sleep hygiene measures (CBT-I) are first-line for insomnia. If pharmacotherapy is needed, low-dose melatonin or ramelteon are safer alternatives. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old man is brought in after his 3rd fall in 4 months. Medication review reveals: lorazepam 1 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Taper and discontinue lorazepam (benzodiazepine); benzodiazepines are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate for older adults due to significantly increased fall risk, cognitive impairment, sedation, and delirium; they are the highest-priority medication class to discontinue for fall prevention", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Benzodiazepines** (lorazepam, diazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam) are among the **highest-risk medication classes for falls** in older adults. The AGS Beers Criteria strongly recommends **avoiding benzodiazepines** in older adults due to: (1) **Increased fall risk** (OR 1.4-1.8), (2) **Cognitive impairment**, (3) **Excessive sedation and psychomotor impairment**, (4) **Delirium risk**, and (5) **Fracture risk** (especially hip fractures). Non-pharmacologic sleep hygiene measures (CBT-I) are first-line for insomnia. If pharmacotherapy is needed, low-dose melatonin or ramelteon are safer alternatives. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female is brought in after her 2nd fall in 12 months. Medication review reveals: zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Discontinue zolpidem (non-benzodiazepine sedative-hypnotic / Z-drug); Z-drugs are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to fall risk similar to benzodiazepines, including excessive sedation, psychomotor impairment, and increased fracture risk especially in older adults", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Non-benzodiazepine receptor agonists (Z-drugs)** (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) bind to the same GABA-A receptor as benzodiazepines and carry **similar fall risks** in older adults. The Beers Criteria recommends avoiding Z-drugs due to: (1) **Falls and fractures** (risk comparable to benzodiazepines), (2) **Excessive sedation and next-morning impairment**, (3) **Complex sleep behaviors** (sleep-driving, sleep-eating). The FDA specifically issued a warning for zolpidem regarding next-morning impairment and recommended lower doses for older adults. Non-pharmacologic CBT-I is preferred for insomnia management. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; FDA Safety Communication; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female is brought in after her 3rd fall in 8 months. Medication review reveals: zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "Discontinue zolpidem (non-benzodiazepine sedative-hypnotic / Z-drug); Z-drugs are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to fall risk similar to benzodiazepines, including excessive sedation, psychomotor impairment, and increased fracture risk especially in older adults", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Non-benzodiazepine receptor agonists (Z-drugs)** (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) bind to the same GABA-A receptor as benzodiazepines and carry **similar fall risks** in older adults. The Beers Criteria recommends avoiding Z-drugs due to: (1) **Falls and fractures** (risk comparable to benzodiazepines), (2) **Excessive sedation and next-morning impairment**, (3) **Complex sleep behaviors** (sleep-driving, sleep-eating). The FDA specifically issued a warning for zolpidem regarding next-morning impairment and recommended lower doses for older adults. Non-pharmacologic CBT-I is preferred for insomnia management. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; FDA Safety Communication; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male is brought in after his 3rd fall in 7 months. Medication review reveals: zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "Discontinue zolpidem (non-benzodiazepine sedative-hypnotic / Z-drug); Z-drugs are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to fall risk similar to benzodiazepines, including excessive sedation, psychomotor impairment, and increased fracture risk especially in older adults", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Non-benzodiazepine receptor agonists (Z-drugs)** (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) bind to the same GABA-A receptor as benzodiazepines and carry **similar fall risks** in older adults. The Beers Criteria recommends avoiding Z-drugs due to: (1) **Falls and fractures** (risk comparable to benzodiazepines), (2) **Excessive sedation and next-morning impairment**, (3) **Complex sleep behaviors** (sleep-driving, sleep-eating). The FDA specifically issued a warning for zolpidem regarding next-morning impairment and recommended lower doses for older adults. Non-pharmacologic CBT-I is preferred for insomnia management. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; FDA Safety Communication; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old female is brought in after her 3rd fall in 7 months. Medication review reveals: lorazepam 1 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "Taper and discontinue lorazepam (benzodiazepine); benzodiazepines are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate for older adults due to significantly increased fall risk, cognitive impairment, sedation, and delirium; they are the highest-priority medication class to discontinue for fall prevention", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Benzodiazepines** (lorazepam, diazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam) are among the **highest-risk medication classes for falls** in older adults. The AGS Beers Criteria strongly recommends **avoiding benzodiazepines** in older adults due to: (1) **Increased fall risk** (OR 1.4-1.8), (2) **Cognitive impairment**, (3) **Excessive sedation and psychomotor impairment**, (4) **Delirium risk**, and (5) **Fracture risk** (especially hip fractures). Non-pharmacologic sleep hygiene measures (CBT-I) are first-line for insomnia. If pharmacotherapy is needed, low-dose melatonin or ramelteon are safer alternatives. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old woman is brought in after her 3rd fall in 6 months. Medication review reveals: oxybutynin 5 mg twice daily for urinary urgency, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Discontinue oxybutynin (first-generation anticholinergic/antimuscarinic); oxybutynin crosses the blood-brain barrier extensively and is listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to cognitive impairment, confusion, sedation, and increased fall risk; consider mirabegron (beta-3 agonist) or behavioral therapies as safer alternatives", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls"]'::jsonb, 1, '**First-generation antimuscarinics** (oxybutynin, tolterodine) have high CNS penetration and cause significant **anticholinergic adverse effects** including cognitive impairment, confusion, blurred vision, constipation, dry mouth, and sedation - all contributing to **increased fall risk**. Oxybutynin is specifically listed in the Beers Criteria. Safer alternatives for overactive bladder include: (1) **Mirabegron** (beta-3 adrenergic agonist, no anticholinergic effects), (2) **Vibegron**, (3) **Bladder training and behavioral therapies** (first-line non-pharmacologic), (4) If an antimuscarinic is needed, use a more bladder-selective agent with lower CNS penetration (e.g., darifenacin, fesoterodine). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 78)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old woman is brought in after her 4th fall in 11 months. Medication review reveals: zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["Discontinue zolpidem (non-benzodiazepine sedative-hypnotic / Z-drug); Z-drugs are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to fall risk similar to benzodiazepines, including excessive sedation, psychomotor impairment, and increased fracture risk especially in older adults", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use", "Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Non-benzodiazepine receptor agonists (Z-drugs)** (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) bind to the same GABA-A receptor as benzodiazepines and carry **similar fall risks** in older adults. The Beers Criteria recommends avoiding Z-drugs due to: (1) **Falls and fractures** (risk comparable to benzodiazepines), (2) **Excessive sedation and next-morning impairment**, (3) **Complex sleep behaviors** (sleep-driving, sleep-eating). The FDA specifically issued a warning for zolpidem regarding next-morning impairment and recommended lower doses for older adults. Non-pharmacologic CBT-I is preferred for insomnia management. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; FDA Safety Communication; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female is brought in after her 4th fall in 12 months. Medication review reveals: zolpidem 10 mg at bedtime for insomnia, a thiazide diuretic, metformin, and atorvastatin. Which of the following is the most important pharmacologic intervention to reduce fall risk?', '["Start a first-generation antihistamine (diphenhydramine) as a sleep aid to ensure restful sleep and reduce daytime fatigue-related falls", "Add a long-acting benzodiazepine at bedtime to improve sleep quality and reduce nighttime falls", "Increase the thiazide diuretic dose to lower blood pressure further and reduce fall risk", "Discontinue zolpidem (non-benzodiazepine sedative-hypnotic / Z-drug); Z-drugs are listed in the Beers Criteria as potentially inappropriate due to fall risk similar to benzodiazepines, including excessive sedation, psychomotor impairment, and increased fracture risk especially in older adults", "No medication changes are needed; falls are an inevitable consequence of aging and are not related to medication use"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Non-benzodiazepine receptor agonists (Z-drugs)** (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone) bind to the same GABA-A receptor as benzodiazepines and carry **similar fall risks** in older adults. The Beers Criteria recommends avoiding Z-drugs due to: (1) **Falls and fractures** (risk comparable to benzodiazepines), (2) **Excessive sedation and next-morning impairment**, (3) **Complex sleep behaviors** (sleep-driving, sleep-eating). The FDA specifically issued a warning for zolpidem regarding next-morning impairment and recommended lower doses for older adults. Non-pharmacologic CBT-I is preferred for insomnia management. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; FDA Safety Communication; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female who lives alone has had 4 falls in the past year, primarily occurring inside her home. An occupational therapist is consulted for a home safety evaluation. Which of the following home modification strategies has the strongest evidence for reducing fall risk in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Remove all furniture from the living room to create an open space for walking", "Install wall-to-wall carpeting throughout the house to cushion any future falls", "Install hospital-grade bed rails on all beds to prevent the patient from getting up at night", "No home modifications are effective; the patient should be moved to an assisted living facility immediately", "Remove loose rugs and clutter from walkways, install grab bars in the bathroom and near the toilet, ensure adequate lighting (especially nightlights in hallways and bathrooms), install handrails on all staircases, use non-slip mats in the shower/tub, and ensure the bed height allows easy transfers"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Home safety modification** is a key component of multifactorial fall prevention. A Cochrane review demonstrated that **home hazard assessment and modification by occupational therapists reduces fall rates by approximately 20-30%** in community-dwelling older adults. Evidence-based modifications include: (1) **Remove tripping hazards** (loose rugs, electrical cords, clutter), (2) **Install grab bars** in the bathroom (near toilet, in shower/tub), (3) **Adequate lighting** (nightlights in hallways, bathrooms, bedrooms), (4) **Handrails on all staircases** (bilateral if possible), (5) **Non-slip mats/strips** in bathtubs and showers, (6) **Raised toilet seats** and shower chairs for patients with mobility limitations, (7) **Secure or remove loose carpeting**, (8) **Appropriate bed height** for easy transfers. Bed rails are NOT recommended as they increase the risk of entrapment injury, particularly in cognitively impaired patients. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Clemson 2008; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male who lives alone has had 4 falls in the past year, primarily occurring inside his home. An occupational therapist is consulted for a home safety evaluation. Which of the following home modification strategies has the strongest evidence for reducing fall risk in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Remove loose rugs and clutter from walkways, install grab bars in the bathroom and near the toilet, ensure adequate lighting (especially nightlights in hallways and bathrooms), install handrails on all staircases, use non-slip mats in the shower/tub, and ensure the bed height allows easy transfers", "Install hospital-grade bed rails on all beds to prevent the patient from getting up at night", "Install wall-to-wall carpeting throughout the house to cushion any future falls", "Remove all furniture from the living room to create an open space for walking", "No home modifications are effective; the patient should be moved to an assisted living facility immediately"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Home safety modification** is a key component of multifactorial fall prevention. A Cochrane review demonstrated that **home hazard assessment and modification by occupational therapists reduces fall rates by approximately 20-30%** in community-dwelling older adults. Evidence-based modifications include: (1) **Remove tripping hazards** (loose rugs, electrical cords, clutter), (2) **Install grab bars** in the bathroom (near toilet, in shower/tub), (3) **Adequate lighting** (nightlights in hallways, bathrooms, bedrooms), (4) **Handrails on all staircases** (bilateral if possible), (5) **Non-slip mats/strips** in bathtubs and showers, (6) **Raised toilet seats** and shower chairs for patients with mobility limitations, (7) **Secure or remove loose carpeting**, (8) **Appropriate bed height** for easy transfers. Bed rails are NOT recommended as they increase the risk of entrapment injury, particularly in cognitively impaired patients. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Clemson 2008; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
