    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old female with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and Parkinson disease reports recurrent falls, primarily occurring when she stands up from a seated or lying position. Blood pressure measured supine is 168/78 mmHg, and upon standing it drops to 142/58 mmHg with dizziness. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate initial management?', '["Orthostatic hypotension (drop of >= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing); management includes reviewing and reducing antihypertensive doses, adequate hydration, rising slowly from supine/seated positions, compression stockings, and considering midodrine or fludrocortisone if non-pharmacologic measures fail", "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity; immediately refer for pacemaker implantation", "Vasovagal syncope; prescribe a beta-blocker to prevent future episodes", "Postprandial hypotension; restrict all meals to once daily to prevent blood pressure drops", "Essential hypertension with white coat effect; increase the antihypertensive doses because the supine blood pressure is elevated"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined as a sustained drop of **>= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic** blood pressure within **3 minutes of standing** from a supine position. It is a major reversible cause of falls in older adults and is highly prevalent in patients with **autonomic neuropathy** (diabetes, Parkinson disease), **volume depletion**, and **medication-related causes** (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, diuretics). Management: (1) **Review and reduce/discontinue offending medications** (most important step), (2) **Adequate hydration** (salt and fluid intake), (3) **Postural precautions** (rise slowly, sit at bedside before standing, avoid prolonged standing), (4) **Compression stockings** (thigh-high or abdominal binders), (5) If refractory: **midodrine** (alpha-1 agonist) or **fludrocortisone** (