    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and Parkinson disease reports recurrent falls, primarily occurring when she stands up from a seated or lying position. Blood pressure measured supine is 158/78 mmHg, and upon standing it drops to 135/65 mmHg with dizziness. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate initial management?', '["Essential hypertension with white coat effect; increase the antihypertensive doses because the supine blood pressure is elevated", "Vasovagal syncope; prescribe a beta-blocker to prevent future episodes", "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity; immediately refer for pacemaker implantation", "Orthostatic hypotension (drop of >= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing); management includes reviewing and reducing antihypertensive doses, adequate hydration, rising slowly from supine/seated positions, compression stockings, and considering midodrine or fludrocortisone if non-pharmacologic measures fail", "Postprandial hypotension; restrict all meals to once daily to prevent blood pressure drops"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined as a sustained drop of **>= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic** blood pressure within **3 minutes of standing** from a supine position. It is a major reversible cause of falls in older adults and is highly prevalent in patients with **autonomic neuropathy** (diabetes, Parkinson disease), **volume depletion**, and **medication-related causes** (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, diuretics). Management: (1) **Review and reduce/discontinue offending medications** (most important step), (2) **Adequate hydration** (salt and fluid intake), (3) **Postural precautions** (rise slowly, sit at bedside before standing, avoid prolonged standing), (4) **Compression stockings** (thigh-high or abdominal binders), (5) If refractory: **midodrine** (alpha-1 agonist) or **fludrocortisone** (mineralocorticoid). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Consensus Statement, JACC 2018)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and Parkinson disease reports recurrent falls, primarily occurring when he stands up from a seated or lying position. Blood pressure measured supine is 169/79 mmHg, and upon standing it drops to 143/60 mmHg with dizziness. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis and the most appropriate initial management?', '["Carotid sinus hypersensitivity; immediately refer for pacemaker implantation", "Essential hypertension with white coat effect; increase the antihypertensive doses because the supine blood pressure is elevated", "Vasovagal syncope; prescribe a beta-blocker to prevent future episodes", "Orthostatic hypotension (drop of >= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing); management includes reviewing and reducing antihypertensive doses, adequate hydration, rising slowly from supine/seated positions, compression stockings, and considering midodrine or fludrocortisone if non-pharmacologic measures fail", "Postprandial hypotension; restrict all meals to once daily to prevent blood pressure drops"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined as a sustained drop of **>= 20 mmHg systolic or >= 10 mmHg diastolic** blood pressure within **3 minutes of standing** from a supine position. It is a major reversible cause of falls in older adults and is highly prevalent in patients with **autonomic neuropathy** (diabetes, Parkinson disease), **volume depletion**, and **medication-related causes** (antihypertensives, alpha-blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, diuretics). Management: (1) **Review and reduce/discontinue offending medications** (most important step), (2) **Adequate hydration** (salt and fluid intake), (3) **Postural precautions** (rise slowly, sit at bedside before standing, avoid prolonged standing), (4) **Compression stockings** (thigh-high or abdominal binders), (5) If refractory: **midodrine** (alpha-1 agonist) or **fludrocortisone** (mineralocorticoid). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Consensus Statement, JACC 2018)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female with a history of recurrent falls undergoes a comprehensive fall risk assessment. Visual acuity testing reveals bilateral visual impairment. She wears bifocal glasses. Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding the relationship between vision impairment, corrective lenses, and fall risk in older adults?', '["Vision has no significant impact on fall risk; falls are exclusively caused by muscle weakness", "Visual impairment (from cataracts, macular degeneration, glaucoma, or diabetic retinopathy) significantly increases fall risk; multifocal/bifocal lenses can increase fall risk during ambulation (especially on stairs) because the lower near-vision segment distorts depth perception and spatial awareness of the ground; single-lens distance glasses for outdoor walking may reduce falls", "Corrective surgery for cataracts has no impact on fall risk or functional outcomes in older adults", "Visual acuity testing is not a recommended component of fall risk assessment", "Bifocal and multifocal glasses reduce fall risk by improving near vision for obstacle detection"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Visual impairment** is a significant independent risk factor for falls in older adults. Common age-related causes include **cataracts, age-related macular degeneration, glaucoma, and diabetic retinopathy**. An important and often overlooked risk factor is **multifocal/bifocal/progressive lenses**: the lower near-vision segment **impairs depth perception and spatial awareness** of the walking surface, particularly on **stairs and uneven terrain**. The **Visible Trial** demonstrated that providing **single-lens distance glasses for outdoor walking** in older adults who habitually wore multifocals reduced outdoor falls by 40%. **Cataract surgery** has been shown to reduce fall risk by approximately 34%. Comprehensive fall risk assessment must include **visual acuity testing** and referral to ophthalmology when impairment is identified. (Ref: Visible Trial, BMJ 2010; Lord 2006; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
