Visible Trial, BMJ 2010; Lord 2006; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old community-dwelling man has had 4 falls in the past year but sustained no fractures. He is interested in an exercise-based fall prevention program. Which of the following exercise interventions has the strongest evidence for reducing fall rates in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Only aquatic exercises are effective because they eliminate the risk of falling during the exercise itself", "High-intensity interval training (HIIT) is the most effective fall prevention exercise for all older adults", "Multicomponent exercise programs that include balance training, progressive strength training, and gait training (e.g., Tai Chi, Otago Exercise Programme) have the strongest evidence; these programs reduce fall rates by 20-40% when performed regularly", "Exercise-based programs have not been shown to reduce falls in any randomized controlled trials", "Solely stretching and flexibility exercises are sufficient to prevent falls"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Exercise-based fall prevention programs** are one of the most effective interventions for reducing falls in community-dwelling older adults. The strongest evidence supports: (1) **Tai Chi** - reduces falls by 20-40% (multiple RCTs, Cochrane Review), improves balance, proprioception, and lower extremity strength. (2) **Otago Exercise Programme** - a home-based program of progressive strength and balance exercises with walking, shown to reduce falls by 35% in multiple RCTs. (3) **Multicomponent programs** combining **balance training, strength/resistance training, and gait training** are more effective than single-component programs. Key principles: exercises must be **progressive** (increasing difficulty), **sustained** (ongoing, not time-limited), and include a strong **balance challenge** component. Flexibility exercises alone are insufficient. The **Cochrane Review (Sherrington 2019)** confirmed that exercise programs reduce fall rates by 23% overall, with higher-dose balance-challenging programs showing greater benefit. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Sherrington 2019; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old community-dwelling woman has had 4 falls in the past year but sustained no fractures. She is interested in an exercise-based fall prevention program. Which of the following exercise interventions has the strongest evidence for reducing fall rates in community-dwelling older adults?', '["High-intensity interval training (HIIT) is the most effective fall prevention exercise for all older adults", "Only aquatic exercises are effective because they eliminate the risk of falling during the exercise itself", "Exercise-based programs have not been shown to reduce falls in any randomized controlled trials", "Solely stretching and flexibility exercises are sufficient to prevent falls", "Multicomponent exercise programs that include balance training, progressive strength training, and gait training (e.g., Tai Chi, Otago Exercise Programme) have the strongest evidence; these programs reduce fall rates by 20-40% when performed regularly"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Exercise-based fall prevention programs** are one of the most effective interventions for reducing falls in community-dwelling older adults. The strongest evidence supports: (1) **Tai Chi** - reduces falls by 20-40% (multiple RCTs, Cochrane Review), improves balance, proprioception, and lower extremity strength. (2) **Otago Exercise Programme** - a home-based program of progressive strength and balance exercises with walking, shown to reduce falls by 35% in multiple RCTs. (3) **Multicomponent programs** combining **balance training, strength/resistance training, and gait training** are more effective than single-component programs. Key principles: exercises must be **progressive** (increasing difficulty), **sustained** (ongoing, not time-limited), and include a strong **balance challenge** component. Flexibility exercises alone are insufficient. The **Cochrane Review (Sherrington 2019)** confirmed that exercise programs reduce fall rates by 23% overall, with higher-dose balance-challenging programs showing greater benefit. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Sherrington 2019; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
