howing greater benefit. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Sherrington 2019; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old community-dwelling male has had 4 falls in the past year but sustained no fractures. He is interested in an exercise-based fall prevention program. Which of the following exercise interventions has the strongest evidence for reducing fall rates in community-dwelling older adults?', '["Exercise-based programs have not been shown to reduce falls in any randomized controlled trials", "Solely stretching and flexibility exercises are sufficient to prevent falls", "Only aquatic exercises are effective because they eliminate the risk of falling during the exercise itself", "High-intensity interval training (HIIT) is the most effective fall prevention exercise for all older adults", "Multicomponent exercise programs that include balance training, progressive strength training, and gait training (e.g., Tai Chi, Otago Exercise Programme) have the strongest evidence; these programs reduce fall rates by 20-40% when performed regularly"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Exercise-based fall prevention programs** are one of the most effective interventions for reducing falls in community-dwelling older adults. The strongest evidence supports: (1) **Tai Chi** - reduces falls by 20-40% (multiple RCTs, Cochrane Review), improves balance, proprioception, and lower extremity strength. (2) **Otago Exercise Programme** - a home-based program of progressive strength and balance exercises with walking, shown to reduce falls by 35% in multiple RCTs. (3) **Multicomponent programs** combining **balance training, strength/resistance training, and gait training** are more effective than single-component programs. Key principles: exercises must be **progressive** (increasing difficulty), **sustained** (ongoing, not time-limited), and include a strong **balance challenge** component. Flexibility exercises alone are insufficient. The **Cochrane Review (Sherrington 2019)** confirmed that exercise programs reduce fall rates by 23% overall, with higher-dose balance-challenging programs showing greater benefit. (Ref: Cochrane Review, Sherrington 2019; AGS/BGS Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old woman is seen for a fall risk assessment after 4 falls in 4 months. Current medications include: lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg twice daily, diphenhydramine 50 mg at bedtime as a sleep aid, omeprazole 20 mg daily, and aspirin 81 mg daily. According to the AGS Beers Criteria and fall prevention guidelines, which medication class poses the highest fall risk and should be prioritized for dose reduction or discontinuation?', '["Switch omeprazole to a higher-dose PPI because acid reflux is causing nocturnal awakening and contributing to falls", "Discontinue diphenhydramine (first-generation antihistamine with strong anticholinergic properties); the Beers Criteria recommends avoiding first-generation antihistamines in older adults due to their high anticholinergic burden causing cognitive impairment, confusion, blurred vision, urinary retention, constipation, sedation, and significantly increased fall risk", "No medication changes are needed; the current regimen is entirely appropriate for an older adult", "Double the lisinopril dose to ensure tighter blood pressure control, as hypertension is the primary cause of falls", "Discontinue aspirin 81 mg because it increases bleeding risk from fall-related injuries and has no effect on cardiovascular outcomes"]'::jsonb, 1, '**First-generation antihistamines** (diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, chlorpheniramine) are **highly anticholinergic** and cause excessive **sedation, cognitive impairment, confusion, blurred vision, dry mouth, constipation, and urinary retention** in older adults - all of which contribute to falls. Diphenhydramine is present in many OTC sleep aids and allergy medications. The Beers Criteria strongly recommends avoidance. Safer alternatives for insomnia: CBT-I, sleep hygiene, low-dose melatonin. For allergies: second-generation antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old woman is seen for a fall risk assessment after 3 falls in 9 months. Current medications include: lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg twice daily, chlorpromazine 25 mg at bedtime for agitation, omeprazole 20 mg daily, and aspirin 81 mg daily. According to the AGS Beers Criteria and fall prevention guidelines, which medication class poses the highest fall risk and should be prioritized for dose reduction or discontinuation?', '["Discontinue aspirin 81 mg because it increases bleeding risk from fall-related injuries and has no effect on cardiovascular outcomes", "Double the lisinopril dose to ensure tighter blood pressure control, as hypertension is the primary cause of falls", "No medication changes are needed; the current regimen is entirely appropriate for an older adult", "Switch omeprazole to a higher-dose PPI because acid reflux is causing nocturnal awakening and contributing to falls", "Taper and discontinue chlorpromazine (first-generation antipsychotic); the Beers Criteria recommends avoiding antipsychotics for behavioral symptoms of dementia unless non-pharmacologic approaches have failed and the patient poses a danger; antipsychotics increase fall risk through sedation, orthostatic hypotension, and extrapyramidal effects, and carry an FDA boxed warning for increased mortality in elderly dementia patients"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Antipsychotics** (both first-generation like chlorpromazine and second-generation like quetiapine) increase fall risk through multiple mechanisms: **sedation, orthostatic hypotension, extrapyramidal symptoms (akathisia, parkinsonism), and cognitive impairment**. Additionally, antipsychotics carry an **FDA boxed warning for increased mortality** in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis. The Beers Criteria recommends avoiding antipsychotics for behavioral symptoms of dementia unless non-pharmacologic interventions have failed and the patient is a danger to self or others. Non-pharmacologic approaches (behavioral modification, environmental changes, caregiver education) are first-line. (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; FDA Boxed Warning; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old man is seen for a fall risk assessment after 2 falls in 6 months. Current medications include: lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg twice daily, diazepam 5 mg twice daily for anxiety, omeprazole 20 mg daily, and aspirin 81 mg daily. According to the AGS Beers Criteria and fall prevention guidelines, which medication class poses the highest fall risk and should be prioritized for dose reduction or discontinuation?', '["Taper and discontinue diazepam (long-acting benzodiazepine); the Beers Criteria strongly recommends avoiding all benzodiazepines in older adults, especially long-acting agents like diazepam (half-life 20-100 hours and active metabolites), due to significantly increased risks of falls, fractures, cognitive impairment, delirium, and oversedation", "No medication changes are needed; the current regimen is entirely appropriate for an older adult", "Switch omeprazole to a higher-dose PPI because acid reflux is causing nocturnal awakening and contributing to falls", "Discontinue aspirin 81 mg because it increases bleeding risk from fall-related injuries and has no effect on cardiovascular outcomes", "Double the lisinopril dose to ensure tighter blood pressure control, as hypertension is the primary cause of falls"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Long-acting benzodiazepines** (diazepam, chlordiazepoxide, flurazepam, clonazepam) are among the **most dangerous medications** for falls in older adults due to their prolonged half-lives and accumulation of active metabolites. Diazepam has a half-life of **20-100 hours** in the elderly. The Beers Criteria identifies ALL benzodiazepines as **potentially inappropriate** regardless of indication. Evidence shows benzodiazepines increase fall risk by **40-80%** and hip fracture risk significantly. First-line alternatives for anxiety: CBT, SSRIs (e.g., sertraline), or SNRIs (e.g., venlafaxine). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines)'),
