    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old man is seen for a fall risk assessment after 3 falls in 8 months. Current medications include: lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg twice daily, diazepam 5 mg twice daily for anxiety, omeprazole 20 mg daily, and aspirin 81 mg daily. According to the AGS Beers Criteria and fall prevention guidelines, which medication class poses the highest fall risk and should be prioritized for dose reduction or discontinuation?', '["Discontinue aspirin 81 mg because it increases bleeding risk from fall-related injuries and has no effect on cardiovascular outcomes", "Taper and discontinue diazepam (long-acting benzodiazepine); the Beers Criteria strongly recommends avoiding all benzodiazepines in older adults, especially long-acting agents like diazepam (half-life 20-100 hours and active metabolites), due to significantly increased risks of falls, fractures, cognitive impairment, delirium, and oversedation", "Switch omeprazole to a higher-dose PPI because acid reflux is causing nocturnal awakening and contributing to falls", "Double the lisinopril dose to ensure tighter blood pressure control, as hypertension is the primary cause of falls", "No medication changes are needed; the current regimen is entirely appropriate for an older adult"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Long-acting benzodiazepines** (diazepam, chlordiazepoxide, flurazepam, clonazepam) are among the **most dangerous medications** for falls in older adults due to their prolonged half-lives and accumulation of active metabolites. Diazepam has a half-life of **20-100 hours** in the elderly. The Beers Criteria identifies ALL benzodiazepines as **potentially inappropriate** regardless of indication. Evidence shows benzodiazepines increase fall risk by **40-80%** and hip fracture risk significantly. First-line alternatives for anxiety: CBT, SSRIs (e.g., sertraline), or SNRIs (e.g., venlafaxine). (Ref: AGS Beers Criteria 2023; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old male presents with a low-trauma vertebral compression fracture. He has a history of chronic prednisone use (10 mg/day for 5 years) for COPD. A DEXA scan reveals a T-score of -2.8 at the lumbar spine. Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding osteoporosis in men?', '["Testosterone replacement alone is sufficient treatment for all male osteoporosis regardless of the underlying cause", "Only women should be treated for osteoporosis; male bone loss is always benign and self-limited", "DEXA T-score criteria cannot be applied to men; a bone biopsy is required for diagnosis in all males", "Osteoporosis does not occur in men because testosterone completely protects bone density throughout life", "Osteoporosis in men is under-recognized; secondary causes account for up to 50-80% of male osteoporosis and must be systematically excluded (hypogonadism, glucocorticoid use, alcohol abuse, antiandrogen therapy); treatment includes bisphosphonates, denosumab, or teriparatide, with the same T-score diagnostic thresholds as for women aged >= 50"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Male osteoporosis** is significantly **under-recognized and under-treated**. Key facts: (1) About **30% of all hip fractures** occur in men, and men have **higher post-hip fracture mortality** than women. (2) **Secondary causes** account for **50-80%** of male osteoporosis (vs. ~30% in women). Common secondary causes include: **hypogonadism** (most common), **glucocorticoid use**, **excessive alcohol use** (>3 drinks/day), **androgen deprivation therapy** for prostate cancer, **malabsorption syndromes**, **hyperparathyroidism**, and **hyperthyroidism**. (3) WHO T-score criteria apply to men **>= 50 years** (osteoporosis = T-score <= -2.5). For men < 50, the Z-score is preferred. (4) Treatment: bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid), denosumab, and teriparatide are all FDA-approved for male osteoporosis. Testosterone replacement may be considered for hypogonadal men but is NOT sufficient as sole therapy if severe osteoporosis/fractures are present. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society Guidelines 2012; AACE/ACE 2020)'),
