    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old postmenopausal female is diagnosed with osteoporosis (T-score -2.9 at the femoral neck). She has no contraindications to any osteoporosis medication. She asks about the overall treatment approach and the sequence of available therapies. Which of the following most accurately describes the current evidence-based treatment algorithm for postmenopausal osteoporosis?', '["First-line: oral bisphosphonate (alendronate or risedronate) plus calcium and vitamin D for all patients; IV zoledronic acid if oral bisphosphonates are contraindicated or not tolerated; denosumab as an alternative anti-resorptive; teriparatide or romosozumab reserved for very high-risk patients (very low T-score, multiple fractures, treatment failure); raloxifene considered if concurrent breast cancer risk reduction is desired", "Teriparatide is always first-line for all patients with osteoporosis regardless of severity", "Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with estrogen plus progesterone is the recommended first-line treatment for all postmenopausal osteoporosis", "Raloxifene is the preferred first-line agent for all postmenopausal women with osteoporosis because it reduces both vertebral and hip fracture risk", "Denosumab is always first-line because it has the fewest side effects of any osteoporosis medication"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The evidence-based **treatment algorithm for postmenopausal osteoporosis**: **First-line**: Oral bisphosphonates (**alendronate** or **risedronate**) are first-line for most patients due to strong fracture reduction evidence (vertebral, non-vertebral, and hip), long track record, generic availability, and cost-effectiveness. **Calcium (1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (800-1000 IU/day)** are adjunctive for all patients. **Second-line/alternatives**: (1) **IV zoledronic acid** - for patients who cannot tolerate or are contraindicated for oral bisphosphonates. (2) **Denosumab** - for patients with renal insufficiency (eGFR < 35 mL/min, where bisphosphonates are relatively contraindicated), intolerance to bisphosphonates, or preference for SC injection. (3) **Raloxifene** - for patients with concurrent high breast cancer risk (reduces only vertebral fractures). **Reserved for very high risk**: (4) **Teriparatide** or **abaloparatide** (anabolic agents) - for very low T-scores, multiple fractures, or treatment failure. (5) **Romosozumab** - for very high fracture risk without CV contraindications. **HRT** is no longer first-line due to cardiovascular and cancer risks (WHI Trial). (Ref: NOF Clinician''s Guide; AACE/ACE 2020; Hazzard''s Ch. 77; Endocrine Society)'),
